# Behavioral Design Patterns Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines focus on behavioral patterns that define how objects interact and communicate with each other. Behavioral patterns help manage algorithms, relationships, and responsibilities between objects, making complex behavior more manageable and systems more flexible. Apply these patterns when you need to define clear communication protocols between components.

## Observer Pattern
- Use for defining a one-to-many dependency between objects
- Implement when an object's state changes need to be communicated to dependent objects
- Apply when you need to maintain consistency across objects
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Observer interface
  abstract class Observer {
    void update(String message);
  }
  
  // Subject interface
  abstract class Subject {
    void addObserver(Observer observer);
    void removeObserver(Observer observer);
    void notifyObservers();
  }
  
  // Concrete subject
  class MessagePublisher implements Subject {
    final List<Observer> _observers = [];
    String _message = '';
    
    void setMessage(String message) {
      _message = message;
      notifyObservers();
    }
    
    @override
    void addObserver(Observer observer) {
      _observers.add(observer);
    }
    
    @override
    void removeObserver(Observer observer) {
      _observers.remove(observer);
    }
    
    @override
    void notifyObservers() {
      for (final observer in _observers) {
        observer.update(_message);
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete observers
  class MessageDisplay implements Observer {
    final String name;
    
    MessageDisplay(this.name);
    
    @override
    void update(String message) {
      print('$name received: $message');
    }
  }
  
  // Flutter-specific implementation with ChangeNotifier
  class DataModel extends ChangeNotifier {
    String _data = '';
    
    String get data => _data;
    
    void updateData(String newData) {
      _data = newData;
      notifyListeners();
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  Widget buildWithProvider() {
    return ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (_) => DataModel(),
      child: Consumer<DataModel>(
        builder: (context, model, child) {
          return Text('Current data: ${model.data}');
        },
      ),
    );
  }
  ```

## Strategy Pattern
- Use for defining a family of algorithms, encapsulating each one
- Implement when you need to switch between algorithms at runtime
- Apply when you have multiple ways to accomplish the same task
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Strategy interface
  abstract class SortStrategy<T> {
    List<T> sort(List<T> data);
  }
  
  // Concrete strategies
  class AscendingSortStrategy<T extends Comparable> implements SortStrategy<T> {
    @override
    List<T> sort(List<T> data) {
      final result = List<T>.from(data);
      result.sort((a, b) => a.compareTo(b));
      return result;
    }
  }
  
  class DescendingSortStrategy<T extends Comparable> implements SortStrategy<T> {
    @override
    List<T> sort(List<T> data) {
      final result = List<T>.from(data);
      result.sort((a, b) => b.compareTo(a));
      return result;
    }
  }
  
  class RandomSortStrategy<T> implements SortStrategy<T> {
    @override
    List<T> sort(List<T> data) {
      final result = List<T>.from(data);
      result.shuffle();
      return result;
    }
  }
  
  // Context
  class DataSorter<T extends Comparable> {
    SortStrategy<T>? _strategy;
    
    void setStrategy(SortStrategy<T> strategy) {
      _strategy = strategy;
    }
    
    List<T> sort(List<T> data) {
      if (_strategy == null) {
        throw Exception('Sort strategy not set');
      }
      return _strategy!.sort(data);
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  void main() {
    final numbers = [5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3];
    final sorter = DataSorter<int>();
    
    // Use ascending strategy
    sorter.setStrategy(AscendingSortStrategy<int>());
    print(sorter.sort(numbers)); // [1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9]
    
    // Switch to descending strategy
    sorter.setStrategy(DescendingSortStrategy<int>());
    print(sorter.sort(numbers)); // [9, 8, 5, 3, 2, 1]
    
    // Switch to random strategy
    sorter.setStrategy(RandomSortStrategy<int>());
    print(sorter.sort(numbers)); // [Random order]
  }
  ```

## Command Pattern
- Use for encapsulating a request as an object
- Implement when you need to parameterize objects with operations
- Apply when you need to queue, log, or undo operations
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Command interface
  abstract class Command {
    void execute();
    void undo();
  }
  
  // Receiver
  class Document {
    final List<String> lines = [];
    
    void addLine(String line) {
      print('Adding line: $line');
      lines.add(line);
    }
    
    void removeLine(int index) {
      if (index >= 0 && index < lines.length) {
        print('Removing line: ${lines[index]}');
        lines.removeAt(index);
      }
    }
    
    void display() {
      print('Document content:');
      for (int i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        print('${i + 1}: ${lines[i]}');
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete commands
  class AddLineCommand implements Command {
    final Document document;
    final String line;
    
    AddLineCommand(this.document, this.line);
    
    @override
    void execute() {
      document.addLine(line);
    }
    
    @override
    void undo() {
      document.removeLine(document.lines.length - 1);
    }
  }
  
  class RemoveLineCommand implements Command {
    final Document document;
    final int index;
    String? _removedLine;
    
    RemoveLineCommand(this.document, this.index);
    
    @override
    void execute() {
      if (index >= 0 && index < document.lines.length) {
        _removedLine = document.lines[index];
        document.removeLine(index);
      }
    }
    
    @override
    void undo() {
      if (_removedLine != null) {
        document.lines.insert(index, _removedLine!);
        print('Restored line: $_removedLine');
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Invoker
  class DocumentEditor {
    final Document document = Document();
    final List<Command> _history = [];
    int _currentIndex = -1;
    
    void executeCommand(Command command) {
      // Remove any commands that were undone
      if (_currentIndex < _history.length - 1) {
        _history.removeRange(_currentIndex + 1, _history.length);
      }
      
      _history.add(command);
      command.execute();
      _currentIndex++;
    }
    
    void undo() {
      if (_currentIndex >= 0) {
        _history[_currentIndex].undo();
        _currentIndex--;
      } else {
        print('Nothing to undo');
      }
    }
    
    void redo() {
      if (_currentIndex < _history.length - 1) {
        _currentIndex++;
        _history[_currentIndex].execute();
      } else {
        print('Nothing to redo');
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  void main() {
    final editor = DocumentEditor();
    
    // Execute commands
    editor.executeCommand(AddLineCommand(editor.document, 'First line'));
    editor.executeCommand(AddLineCommand(editor.document, 'Second line'));
    editor.executeCommand(AddLineCommand(editor.document, 'Third line'));
    editor.document.display();
    
    // Undo last command
    editor.undo();
    editor.document.display();
    
    // Redo the command
    editor.redo();
    editor.document.display();
    
    // Execute a new command
    editor.executeCommand(RemoveLineCommand(editor.document, 1));
    editor.document.display();
    
    // Undo and redo
    editor.undo();
    editor.document.display();
  }
  ```

## State Pattern
- Use for allowing an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes
- Implement when an object's behavior depends on its state
- Apply when you want to avoid large conditional statements
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // State interface
  abstract class PlayerState {
    void play(AudioPlayer player);
    void pause(AudioPlayer player);
    void stop(AudioPlayer player);
    String get name;
  }
  
  // Context
  class AudioPlayer {
    PlayerState _state = StoppedState();
    
    void setState(PlayerState state) {
      print('Changing state from ${_state.name} to ${state.name}');
      _state = state;
    }
    
    void play() {
      _state.play(this);
    }
    
    void pause() {
      _state.pause(this);
    }
    
    void stop() {
      _state.stop(this);
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete states
  class PlayingState implements PlayerState {
    @override
    void play(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Already playing');
    }
    
    @override
    void pause(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Pausing playback');
      player.setState(PausedState());
    }
    
    @override
    void stop(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Stopping playback');
      player.setState(StoppedState());
    }
    
    @override
    String get name => 'Playing';
  }
  
  class PausedState implements PlayerState {
    @override
    void play(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Resuming playback');
      player.setState(PlayingState());
    }
    
    @override
    void pause(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Already paused');
    }
    
    @override
    void stop(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Stopping playback');
      player.setState(StoppedState());
    }
    
    @override
    String get name => 'Paused';
  }
  
  class StoppedState implements PlayerState {
    @override
    void play(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Starting playback');
      player.setState(PlayingState());
    }
    
    @override
    void pause(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Can\'t pause when stopped');
    }
    
    @override
    void stop(AudioPlayer player) {
      print('Already stopped');
    }
    
    @override
    String get name => 'Stopped';
  }
  
  // Usage with Flutter UI
  class AudioPlayerWidget extends StatefulWidget {
    @override
    _AudioPlayerWidgetState createState() => _AudioPlayerWidgetState();
  }
  
  class _AudioPlayerWidgetState extends State<AudioPlayerWidget> {
    final AudioPlayer _player = AudioPlayer();
    String _stateText = 'Stopped';
    
    void _updateStateText() {
      setState(() {
        _stateText = _player._state.name;
      });
    }
    
    @override
    Widget build(BuildContext context) {
      return Row(
        mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
        children: [
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              _player.play();
              _updateStateText();
            },
            child: Icon(Icons.play_arrow),
          ),
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              _player.pause();
              _updateStateText();
            },
            child: Icon(Icons.pause),
          ),
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              _player.stop();
              _updateStateText();
            },
            child: Icon(Icons.stop),
          ),
          SizedBox(width: 16),
          Text('Player state: $_stateText'),
        ],
      );
    }
  }
  ```

## Template Method Pattern
- Use for defining the skeleton of an algorithm, deferring some steps to subclasses
- Implement when you have an algorithm with variant and invariant parts
- Apply when you want to avoid code duplication across similar algorithms
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Abstract class with template method
  abstract class DataProcessor {
    // Template method
    Future<void> processData() async {
      await loadData();
      transformData();
      validateData();
      await saveData();
      notifyCompletion();
    }
    
    // Abstract methods to be overridden
    Future<void> loadData();
    void transformData();
    
    // Optional hook methods with default implementation
    void validateData() {
      print('Default data validation');
    }
    
    // Concrete methods
    Future<void> saveData() async {
      print('Saving data to database');
      await Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 1));
    }
    
    void notifyCompletion() {
      print('Data processing completed');
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete implementation
  class ImageProcessor extends DataProcessor {
    @override
    Future<void> loadData() async {
      print('Loading image data');
      await Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 1));
    }
    
    @override
    void transformData() {
      print('Applying image filters');
    }
    
    @override
    void validateData() {
      print('Validating image dimensions and format');
    }
  }
  
  // Another concrete implementation
  class TextProcessor extends DataProcessor {
    @override
    Future<void> loadData() async {
      print('Loading text data');
      await Future.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: 500));
    }
    
    @override
    void transformData() {
      print('Formatting and spell-checking text');
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  Future<void> main() async {
    final imageProcessor = ImageProcessor();
    final textProcessor = TextProcessor();
    
    print('Processing image:');
    await imageProcessor.processData();
    
    print('\nProcessing text:');
    await textProcessor.processData();
  }
  ```

## Chain of Responsibility Pattern
- Use for passing a request along a chain of handlers
- Implement when more than one object may handle a request
- Apply when you want to decouple request senders from receivers
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Handler interface
  abstract class RequestHandler {
    RequestHandler? _nextHandler;
    
    RequestHandler setNext(RequestHandler handler) {
      _nextHandler = handler;
      return handler;
    }
    
    Future<String?> handle(Request request);
    
    Future<String?> handleNext(Request request) async {
      if (_nextHandler != null) {
        return _nextHandler!.handle(request);
      }
      return null;
    }
  }
  
  // Request class
  class Request {
    final String type;
    final Map<String, dynamic> data;
    
    Request(this.type, this.data);
  }
  
  // Concrete handlers
  class AuthenticationHandler extends RequestHandler {
    @override
    Future<String?> handle(Request request) async {
      print('AuthenticationHandler checking request...');
      
      if (request.type == 'auth') {
        if (request.data['username'] == 'admin' && request.data['password'] == 'password') {
          return 'Authentication successful';
        } else {
          return 'Authentication failed';
        }
      }
      
      // Pass to next handler
      return handleNext(request);
    }
  }
  
  class DataValidationHandler extends RequestHandler {
    @override
    Future<String?> handle(Request request) async {
      print('DataValidationHandler checking request...');
      
      if (!request.data.containsKey('id')) {
        return 'Validation failed: Missing ID';
      }
      
      // Pass to next handler
      return handleNext(request);
    }
  }
  
  class DatabaseHandler extends RequestHandler {
    @override
    Future<String?> handle(Request request) async {
      print('DatabaseHandler processing request...');
      
      if (request.type == 'data') {
        return 'Data processed and saved to database';
      }
      
      // Pass to next handler
      return handleNext(request);
    }
  }
  
  class LoggingHandler extends RequestHandler {
    @override
    Future<String?> handle(Request request) async {
      print('LoggingHandler logging request of type: ${request.type}');
      
      // Always continue to next handler
      final result = await handleNext(request);
      
      print('Request processed with result: $result');
      return result;
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  Future<void> main() async {
    // Create chain of responsibility
    final logHandler = LoggingHandler();
    final authHandler = AuthenticationHandler();
    final validationHandler = DataValidationHandler();
    final dbHandler = DatabaseHandler();
    
    logHandler.setNext(authHandler).setNext(validationHandler).setNext(dbHandler);
    
    // Process authentication request
    final authRequest = Request('auth', {
      'username': 'admin',
      'password': 'password',
      'id': '123'
    });
    final authResult = await logHandler.handle(authRequest);
    print('Final result: $authResult\n');
    
    // Process data request
    final dataRequest = Request('data', {
      'id': '456',
      'value': 'test data'
    });
    final dataResult = await logHandler.handle(dataRequest);
    print('Final result: $dataResult\n');
    
    // Process invalid request
    final invalidRequest = Request('data', {
      'value': 'missing ID'
    });
    final invalidResult = await logHandler.handle(invalidRequest);
    print('Final result: $invalidResult');
  }
  ```

## Mediator Pattern
- Use for defining an object that encapsulates how a set of objects interact
- Implement when objects communicate in complex ways
- Apply when you want to reduce dependencies between objects
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Mediator interface
  abstract class ChatMediator {
    void sendMessage(String message, User sender);
    void addUser(User user);
  }
  
  // Colleague class
  abstract class User {
    final String name;
    ChatMediator? mediator;
    
    User(this.name);
    
    void send(String message) {
      print('$name sending: $message');
      mediator?.sendMessage(message, this);
    }
    
    void receive(String message) {
      print('$name received: $message');
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete colleague
  class ChatUser extends User {
    ChatUser(String name) : super(name);
  }
  
  // Concrete mediator
  class ChatRoom implements ChatMediator {
    final List<User> users = [];
    
    @override
    void sendMessage(String message, User sender) {
      print('\n${sender.name} sent message to chat room: "$message"');
      
      // Send message to all users except the sender
      for (var user in users) {
        if (user != sender) {
          user.receive(message);
        }
      }
    }
    
    @override
    void addUser(User user) {
      print('${user.name} joined the chat room');
      user.mediator = this;
      users.add(user);
    }
  }
  
  // Flutter UI example
  class ChatRoomWidget extends StatefulWidget {
    @override
    _ChatRoomWidgetState createState() => _ChatRoomWidgetState();
  }
  
  class _ChatRoomWidgetState extends State<ChatRoomWidget> {
    final ChatRoom _chatRoom = ChatRoom();
    final List<String> _messages = [];
    final TextEditingController _controller = TextEditingController();
    late ChatUser _currentUser;
    
    @override
    void initState() {
      super.initState();
      
      // Create users
      _currentUser = ChatUser('You');
      final user1 = ChatUser('Alice');
      final user2 = ChatUser('Bob');
      
      // Add users to chat room
      _chatRoom.addUser(_currentUser);
      _chatRoom.addUser(user1);
      _chatRoom.addUser(user2);
      
      // Override receive to update UI
      _currentUser.receive = (message) {
        setState(() {
          _messages.add('You received: $message');
        });
      };
    }
    
    void _sendMessage() {
      if (_controller.text.isNotEmpty) {
        setState(() {
          _messages.add('You sent: ${_controller.text}');
        });
        _currentUser.send(_controller.text);
        _controller.clear();
      }
    }
    
    @override
    Widget build(BuildContext context) {
      return Column(
        children: [
          Expanded(
            child: ListView.builder(
              itemCount: _messages.length,
              itemBuilder: (context, index) {
                return ListTile(
                  title: Text(_messages[index]),
                );
              },
            ),
          ),
          Padding(
            padding: EdgeInsets.all(8.0),
            child: Row(
              children: [
                Expanded(
                  child: TextField(
                    controller: _controller,
                    decoration: InputDecoration(
                      hintText: 'Enter message',
                    ),
                  ),
                ),
                IconButton(
                  icon: Icon(Icons.send),
                  onPressed: _sendMessage,
                ),
              ],
            ),
          ),
        ],
      );
    }
  }
  ```

## Memento Pattern
- Use for capturing and externalizing an object's internal state
- Implement when you need to restore an object to a previous state
- Apply when direct access to an object's fields violates encapsulation
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Memento class
  class EditorState {
    final String _text;
    final int _cursorPosition;
    final TextSelection? _selection;
    
    EditorState(this._text, this._cursorPosition, this._selection);
    
    String get text => _text;
    int get cursorPosition => _cursorPosition;
    TextSelection? get selection => _selection;
  }
  
  // Originator
  class TextEditor {
    String _text = '';
    int _cursorPosition = 0;
    TextSelection? _selection;
    
    String getText() => _text;
    
    void setText(String text) {
      _text = text;
      _cursorPosition = text.length;
      _selection = null;
    }
    
    void setCursorPosition(int position) {
      if (position >= 0 && position <= _text.length) {
        _cursorPosition = position;
        _selection = null;
      }
    }
    
    void setSelection(int start, int end) {
      if (start >= 0 && end <= _text.length && start <= end) {
        _selection = TextSelection(baseOffset: start, extentOffset: end);
      }
    }
    
    // Create a memento
    EditorState save() {
      return EditorState(_text, _cursorPosition, _selection);
    }
    
    // Restore from memento
    void restore(EditorState state) {
      _text = state.text;
      _cursorPosition = state.cursorPosition;
      _selection = state.selection;
    }
    
    @override
    String toString() {
      return 'Text: "$_text", Cursor: $_cursorPosition, Selection: $_selection';
    }
  }
  
  // Caretaker
  class History {
    final List<EditorState> _states = [];
    int _currentStateIndex = -1;
    
    void save(TextEditor editor) {
      // Remove any forward history if we're not at the end
      if (_currentStateIndex < _states.length - 1) {
        _states.removeRange(_currentStateIndex + 1, _states.length);
      }
      
      // Save current state
      _states.add(editor.save());
      _currentStateIndex++;
      
      print('Saved state $_currentStateIndex: ${editor.toString()}');
    }
    
    bool canUndo() => _currentStateIndex >= 0;
    
    bool canRedo() => _currentStateIndex < _states.length - 1;
    
    void undo(TextEditor editor) {
      if (canUndo()) {
        _currentStateIndex--;
        editor.restore(_states[_currentStateIndex]);
        print('Undo to state $_currentStateIndex: ${editor.toString()}');
      } else {
        print('Cannot undo, at earliest state');
      }
    }
    
    void redo(TextEditor editor) {
      if (canRedo()) {
        _currentStateIndex++;
        editor.restore(_states[_currentStateIndex]);
        print('Redo to state $_currentStateIndex: ${editor.toString()}');
      } else {
        print('Cannot redo, at latest state');
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  void main() {
    final editor = TextEditor();
    final history = History();
    
    // Initial state
    editor.setText('Hello');
    history.save(editor);
    
    // Make changes
    editor.setText('Hello, World');
    history.save(editor);
    
    editor.setText('Hello, World!');
    editor.setSelection(7, 12);
    history.save(editor);
    
    // Undo changes
    history.undo(editor);
    history.undo(editor);
    
    // Redo changes
    history.redo(editor);
    
    // Make new change, which discards forward history
    editor.setText('New direction');
    history.save(editor);
    
    // Try to redo (should fail)
    history.redo(editor);
  }
  ```

## Visitor Pattern
- Use for representing operations to be performed on elements of an object structure
- Implement when you need to separate algorithms from the objects they operate on
- Apply when you need to add new operations to existing objects without modifying them
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Element interface
  abstract class UIElement {
    void accept(UIVisitor visitor);
  }
  
  // Visitor interface
  abstract class UIVisitor {
    void visitButton(Button element);
    void visitTextField(TextField element);
    void visitCheckbox(Checkbox element);
  }
  
  // Concrete elements
  class Button implements UIElement {
    final String text;
    
    Button(this.text);
    
    @override
    void accept(UIVisitor visitor) {
      visitor.visitButton(this);
    }
  }
  
  class TextField implements UIElement {
    final String hint;
    String value = '';
    
    TextField(this.hint);
    
    @override
    void accept(UIVisitor visitor) {
      visitor.visitTextField(this);
    }
  }
  
  class Checkbox implements UIElement {
    final String label;
    bool checked = false;
    
    Checkbox(this.label);
    
    @override
    void accept(UIVisitor visitor) {
      visitor.visitCheckbox(this);
    }
  }
  
  // Concrete visitors
  class RenderVisitor implements UIVisitor {
    @override
    void visitButton(Button element) {
      print('Rendering Button with text: ${element.text}');
    }
    
    @override
    void visitTextField(TextField element) {
      print('Rendering TextField with hint: ${element.hint}, value: ${element.value}');
    }
    
    @override
    void visitCheckbox(Checkbox element) {
      print('Rendering Checkbox with label: ${element.label}, checked: ${element.checked}');
    }
  }
  
  class AccessibilityVisitor implements UIVisitor {
    @override
    void visitButton(Button element) {
      print('Accessibility for Button: Adding role="button" and label="${element.text}"');
    }
    
    @override
    void visitTextField(TextField element) {
      print('Accessibility for TextField: Adding role="textbox" and placeholder="${element.hint}"');
    }
    
    @override
    void visitCheckbox(Checkbox element) {
      print('Accessibility for Checkbox: Adding role="checkbox", label="${element.label}", checked=${element.checked}');
    }
  }
  
  class ValidationVisitor implements UIVisitor {
    @override
    void visitButton(Button element) {
      if (element.text.isEmpty) {
        print('Validation error: Button text cannot be empty');
      }
    }
    
    @override
    void visitTextField(TextField element) {
      // Example validation for email field
      if (element.hint.contains('email') && element.value.isNotEmpty) {
        if (!element.value.contains('@')) {
          print('Validation error: Invalid email format');
        }
      }
    }
    
    @override
    void visitCheckbox(Checkbox element) {
      // No validation needed for checkbox
    }
  }
  
  // Object structure
  class Form {
    final List<UIElement> elements = [];
    
    void addElement(UIElement element) {
      elements.add(element);
    }
    
    void accept(UIVisitor visitor) {
      for (var element in elements) {
        element.accept(visitor);
      }
    }
  }
  
  // Usage
  void main() {
    // Create elements
    final submitButton = Button('Submit');
    final emailField = TextField('Enter email');
    emailField.value = 'test.example.com'; // Invalid email
    final termsCheckbox = Checkbox('I agree to terms');
    termsCheckbox.checked = true;
    
    // Create form with elements
    final form = Form();
    form.addElement(emailField);
    form.addElement(termsCheckbox);
    form.addElement(submitButton);
    
    // Apply different visitors
    print('=== Rendering UI ===');
    form.accept(RenderVisitor());
    
    print('\n=== Adding Accessibility ===');
    form.accept(AccessibilityVisitor());
    
    print('\n=== Validating Form ===');
    form.accept(ValidationVisitor());
  }
  ```

## File Structure Best Practices
- Group related behavioral patterns by feature area
- Place interfaces and base classes in separate files
- Example file organization:
  ```
  lib/
    core/
      behaviors/
        observers/
          observer_base.dart
        commands/
          command_base.dart
        states/
          state_machine.dart
    domain/
      usecases/
        template_methods/
          data_processing.dart
    features/
      editor/
        memento/
          editor_history.dart
        commands/
          text_commands.dart
      messaging/
        mediator/
          chat_room.dart
      forms/
        visitors/
          form_validation.dart
  ```

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Use command pattern for undo/redo operations but limit history size
- Implement flyweight for shared state in the state pattern
- Optimize mediator to avoid unnecessary notifications
- Cache visitor results when appropriate
- Use lazy initialization in chain of responsibility handlers
- Batch updates in observer pattern to avoid frequent UI rebuilds
- Control recursion depth in composite-visitor combinations
- Consider using weak references for observers to avoid memory leaks

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Test each pattern implementation independently
- Verify state transitions in state pattern
- Test undo/redo functionality in command pattern
- Verify that all handlers in chain of responsibility work correctly
- Test visitor implementations with different element combinations
- Verify that observers are properly notified of changes
- Test mediator with different communication scenarios
- Check that template method steps are called in the correct order
